history.feedback:
True to line 6 ('The rain has come, rest has arrived... The noble one advancing: adverse'). The problems are that it doesn't land in two seconds and it clashes with the rest of the short. (1) 'The rain' only works if the viewer knows the Judgment's 'dense clouds but no rain'. The hook says 'nothing happens' but never mentions rain, so the viewer has nothing to hang the rain on. (2) The short just said 'not yet, work on yourself', so 'stop pushing' reads as more of the same advice. The line's actual twist, that you rest once the rain comes, gets lost. (3) 'Pushing' is not in the text; it says rest and not advancing. Better options: (a) keep line 6 and say it plainly, e.g. 'Rain at last:
time to rest.' (b) Use line 3, the most concrete and funny image in the hexagram: 'Wheels come off;
the couple glares.' (16/17 chars), which also fits a lit-line picture.
